Yeah. And seeding an audience can start with giving one person a role in the scene. You show up overdressed to a backyard barbecue and tell the host, “I’m here to inspect the grill.” If he hands you the tongs and says, “We’ve been expecting you,” now there’s a character. He helped make it.

That’s where it can get away from you, too. Once people know the character, they can call him back without your permission. You made one joke at the cookout. Three summers later, somebody hands you the tongs before you’ve even said hello.

You chose the costume. They decided it was a recurring role.